  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 407 PM EDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nGoing into the start of the upcoming workweek, the upper-level \npattern will be fairly zonal, with surface high pressure lingering \nover the area. While ample amounts of instability will be present \n(>2500 J/kg of mean SBCAPE per latest LREF), weak forcing and shear \nwill again be a limiting factor in the production of showers and \nstorms. Southerly near-surface flow will allow for very warm and \nhumid air to linger over the region towards the middle of the \nworkweek. Highs in the low 90s with dew points in the low-to-mid 70s \nare forecast, allowing afternoon heat indices to peak in the upper \n90s/reaching 100. While this is currently expected to remain below \nadvisory criteria, conditions will be quite muggy regardless over \nthis stretch. Overnight relief will be limited, with temperatures \nonly dropping down to the low-to-mid 70s. \n\nWith the zonal pattern overhead and the main jet remaining off to \nthe north for the early part of the week, most of the shortwaves and \nperturbations that pass by that could produce showers and storms \nwill also stay off to the north. This won't last for long though as \nthe pattern begins to shift on Tuesday. The upper-level pattern will \namplify some, with the western CONUS ridge building and troughing \nover the eastern CONUS digging. This will allow the flow over our \nregion to shift to a northwesterly regime, as well as the southern \nperiphery of the jet to move towards the region as well. With \nnumerous shortwaves expected to trek towards the Midwest and Ohio \nValley, chances for showers and storms will increase starting \nTuesday night. The coverage initially will be in north central \nKentucky and southern Indiana, slowly shifting southward as the \ntrough continues to dig southward. With model soundings continuing \nto show elevated instability and a modest wind shear profile, strong \nto severe storms cannot be completely ruled out with each passing \nwave. The potential for excessive rainfall is also possible as any \nstronger storms that do develop could produce heavier downpours.\n\nA cold front will move over the area by late week, proving a well-\nneeded reprieve from the hot and humid conditions over the region. \nThe progression and placement of the front by the end of the \nforecast period is uncertain based off the most recent ensemble \nguidance, resulting in additional uncertainty in precip chances \nduring that portion of the forecast period.",
